{"connection_id": "BC-1a2b6b312f482832e3c4", "ref": "Didache 10.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thanksgiving for the holy name dwelling in hearts", "note": "The eucharistic prayer thanks the Father for his holy name dwelling in believers' hearts; this joins prayer, gratitude, God's name, and inward remembrance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e68585e4fff2ef9bd85", "ref": "Didache 8.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hallowing God's name in the Lord's Prayer", "note": "The Didache prescribes the Lord's Prayer, which asks that the Father's name be hallowed; this is a concrete Christian liturgical form of praise within prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-51c085af138040152f4d", "ref": "Didache 8.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Lord's Prayer formula; hallowing the Name", "note": "The Didache transmits the Lord's Prayer as the form believers are to pray, including the hallowing of the Father's name. It supplies the early Christian liturgical text that its following thrice-daily rule presupposes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d2a5249ff5e90b96731c", "ref": "Didache 8.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer repeated three times daily", "note": "The Didache tells its readers to pray this prayer three times a day; this shows remembrance and praise of the name as repeated verbal practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d9192f0a3e30ce5a83a", "ref": "Didache 8.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "reciting the Lord's Prayer three times daily", "note": "The Didache turns the Lord's Prayer into a thrice-daily discipline. This early Christian rule offers a concrete liturgical counterpart to the commentary's repeated prayer and remembrance around the day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-875ec8347dcc1f6cbd2a", "ref": "Justin Martyr, First Apology 67", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "reading, exhortation, then common prayer", "note": "Justin describes Scripture being read, the leader exhorting hearers, and all standing together to pray; this concrete service sequence matches the commentary's reading, reminder, and prayer as one practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d0d2e7cdfc82ed6ec50", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praying and singing with spirit and mind", "note": "Paul says he will pray and sing both with spirit and with mind; this concretely unites verbal prayer, praise, and inward intention."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a52c745998ee896d47e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praying and singing praise with spirit and mind", "note": "Paul deliberately pairs prayer and praise in both spirit and mind, providing a close Christian counterpart to the commentary's unified tesbih, remembrance, and prayer with intentional participation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a850696198ddd90856f4", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "an audible blessing needs an intelligible Amen", "note": "Paul asks how an outsider can say Amen to a blessing if he does not know what was said; this makes the public, voiced character of liturgical prayer explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-027588cae36301391c10", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.25", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "secrets of the heart; falling face-down; worship and confession", "note": "When hidden heart-secrets are disclosed, the hearer falls on his face, worships God, and declares God truly present. This unusually combines the commentary's hidden/open axis, prostration, and voiced God-remembrance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4eec2e45cc973b5ff897", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.1.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "confession, forgiveness, cleansing", "note": "God forgives and cleanses those who confess; it provides a specific Christian pairing of spoken penitential address and divine purification relevant to the commentary's 14–15 relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-afba0c2e922cfbd7845a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unceasing prayer", "note": "Paul's short command to pray continually makes devotion an ongoing practice, alongside the commentary's recurring remembrance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-29f6a419b91f4ac9dddd", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unceasing prayer", "note": "The command to pray without ceasing provides a Christian analogue to the commentary's repeated, abundant tesbih and remembrance, although it does not prescribe the same ritual form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74d7a6e7418bcc58324a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.18", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thanksgiving in every circumstance", "note": "The next command is to give thanks in everything; this makes thanksgiving an explicit companion to continual prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37a07015871100dbb5f6", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.2.1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "supplications, prayers, intercessions, thanksgivings", "note": "The verse deliberately lists supplications, prayers, intercessions, and thanksgivings. It supplies a close Christian comparison to the commentary's semantic expansion of salat and dhikr into du'a, praise, and thanks."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39e5460e5d7488e41f4a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.2.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "holy hands lifted in prayer", "note": "Paul directs men to pray everywhere, lifting holy hands; this is a specific posture for the bodily prayer described in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72374478570b7fd446bd", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.2.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer everywhere; lifted holy hands; moral restraint", "note": "The instruction to pray everywhere with lifted holy hands is joined to rejection of anger and disputing. It connects the commentary's bodily prayer with its ethical claim that prayer opposes wrongdoing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-81d1630e259c342c1637", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.10.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayers and alms as a memorial before God", "note": "Cornelius's prayers and alms ascend as a memorial before God. The passage gives a striking New Testament link between remembrance, prayer, and obedient action, matching the commentary's broad account of dhikr."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71254b806f32fa007128", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.16.25", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer joined to hymn-singing", "note": "At midnight Paul and Silas are praying and singing hymns to God; this concretely treats supplication and praise as one devotional act."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf42d0ab69fe64261669", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.16.25", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praying and hymn-singing heard by others", "note": "Paul and Silas pray and sing hymns at midnight, with prisoners listening; this is a specific scene of the vocal, audible devotional practice developed from jahr in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d9750eee99d59f0db988", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.2.21", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "calling on the Lord's name", "note": "The verse promises salvation to everyone who calls on the Lord's name; this treats invocation of the name as an act of devotion, as in 87:15."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dfba85ec2d02b4444c09", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.2.42", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teaching and prayers in the community", "note": "The first believers devote themselves to apostolic teaching and to prayers; this places recitation/admonition and prayer in the same communal practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-900274f514344b0a81d2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.22.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "washing away sins while calling on the Name", "note": "Paul is told to be washed from sins while calling on the Lord's name. It specifically joins name-invocation to cleansing, a close Christian meeting of the commentary's remembrance and divine purification relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0fe1f334cf3d67e25170", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.3.1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the ninth-hour time of prayer", "note": "Peter and John go up together to the temple at the appointed hour of prayer. The scene offers a specific communal, timed counterpart to the commentary's recurring prayer and praise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71994da0d55cafd1e26e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.59", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "calling on Jesus by name", "note": "Stephen calls on the Lord Jesus as he entrusts his spirit to him; this is a specific invocation of the Lord's name within prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f662ae377d84d9f53bd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.60", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kneeling and crying loudly in prayer", "note": "Stephen kneels and cries with a loud voice for his killers' forgiveness; this joins bodily posture, audible prayer, and supplication."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16f8be3bf109e3a308a3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word, teaching, song, and gratitude", "note": "Christ's word is to dwell richly as believers teach and admonish one another through psalms and hymns, singing with gratitude; this closely matches the commentary's sequence of recitation, reminder, and prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a37e0435edae2823539f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word dwelling, teaching and admonition, psalms and gratitude", "note": "The verse puts instruction and admonition together with psalms, hymns, spiritual songs, and gratitude; it closely resembles the commentary's intervening recitation, reminder, and worship as one practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-828394334708e02ada68", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word and deed in the Name; thanksgiving", "note": "Whatever is done in word or deed is to be done in the Lord's name with thanks. This directly mirrors the commentary's integration of utterance, deed, Name, and gratitude under worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0e4e1ab6b1295858d20f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.4.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer watchfulness with thanksgiving", "note": "The community is told to persist in prayer, watchful with thanksgiving; this is a specific pairing of supplication and praise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0c8c6d1f72a024f06b59", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.5.19", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "psalms, hymns, and singing", "note": "The community is told to speak to one another in psalms and hymns and sing to the Lord; this shows verbal recitation and song as shared worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-32de1da7ed096f4cd44c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.5.19", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "psalms and hymns spoken and sung in the heart", "note": "The exhortation joins speaking psalms and hymns with singing in the heart to the Lord; it reflects the commentary's double focus on vocal remembrance and inward devotion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc55f3b846d98e270cf4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.5.20", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thanks in Jesus' name", "note": "The next verse calls for continual thanks to God the Father in Jesus' name; this connects praise and remembrance with prayerful gratitude."}
{"connection_id": "BC-436fe931da86af05a6d8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.22", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "drawing near; hearts sprinkled clean; bodies washed", "note": "Hebrews couples approach to God with a true heart and imagery of cleansed hearts and washed bodies. It provides a Christian purification-and-worship analogue to the commentary's 87:14–15 association."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1f9d3e58a3b1a099ec0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praise from lips confessing the name", "note": "The passage calls praise a sacrifice offered to God, the fruit of lips confessing his name; this directly joins verbal praise, the divine name, and worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e62b8d4527de377702d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sacrifice of praise; fruit of lips confessing the Name", "note": "Hebrews calls continual praise the fruit of lips that confess God's name; it closely matches the commentary's dhikr as the Name on the tongue and tesbih as worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cbc149ebcce22b2a5609", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "doing good and sharing as sacrifices pleasing to God", "note": "This verse calls concrete beneficence and sharing sacrifices pleasing to God. It extends the commentary's word-action-intention account of worship to an explicitly ethical Christian practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c17c51993a8df3fd4625", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer in suffering, praise in joy", "note": "James directs the suffering person to pray and the cheerful person to sing praise; this shows prayer and praise as closely related responses within lived practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7ffb486b87088341479", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.11.41", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus thanks the Father aloud", "note": "Before raising Lazarus, Jesus looks upward and thanks the Father; this is audible prayer in a public scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3ef4ea40fff3fbe585bf", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.11.42", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "public prayer spoken for hearers", "note": "Jesus explains that he spoke for the surrounding crowd to believe; this qualifies the warning against performative prayer by showing an aloud prayer directed to others' understanding."}

