{"connection_id": "BC-7e68585e4fff2ef9bd85", "ref": "Didache 8.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hallowing God's name in the Lord's Prayer", "note": "The Didache prescribes the Lord's Prayer, which asks that the Father's name be hallowed; this is a concrete Christian liturgical form of praise within prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-51c085af138040152f4d", "ref": "Didache 8.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Lord's Prayer formula; hallowing the Name", "note": "The Didache transmits the Lord's Prayer as the form believers are to pray, including the hallowing of the Father's name. It supplies the early Christian liturgical text that its following thrice-daily rule presupposes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d2a5249ff5e90b96731c", "ref": "Didache 8.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer repeated three times daily", "note": "The Didache tells its readers to pray this prayer three times a day; this shows remembrance and praise of the name as repeated verbal practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d9192f0a3e30ce5a83a", "ref": "Didache 8.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "reciting the Lord's Prayer three times daily", "note": "The Didache turns the Lord's Prayer into a thrice-daily discipline. This early Christian rule offers a concrete liturgical counterpart to the commentary's repeated prayer and remembrance around the day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-875ec8347dcc1f6cbd2a", "ref": "Justin Martyr, First Apology 67", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "reading, exhortation, then common prayer", "note": "Justin describes Scripture being read, the leader exhorting hearers, and all standing together to pray; this concrete service sequence matches the commentary's reading, reminder, and prayer as one practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d0d2e7cdfc82ed6ec50", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praying and singing with spirit and mind", "note": "Paul says he will pray and sing both with spirit and with mind; this concretely unites verbal prayer, praise, and inward intention."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a52c745998ee896d47e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praying and singing praise with spirit and mind", "note": "Paul deliberately pairs prayer and praise in both spirit and mind, providing a close Christian counterpart to the commentary's unified tesbih, remembrance, and prayer with intentional participation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a850696198ddd90856f4", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "an audible blessing needs an intelligible Amen", "note": "Paul asks how an outsider can say Amen to a blessing if he does not know what was said; this makes the public, voiced character of liturgical prayer explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-027588cae36301391c10", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.25", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "secrets of the heart; falling face-down; worship and confession", "note": "When hidden heart-secrets are disclosed, the hearer falls on his face, worships God, and declares God truly present. This unusually combines the commentary's hidden/open axis, prostration, and voiced God-remembrance."}
