{"connection_id": "BC-9aa9d1ca91f4f7025acb", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.23", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "worship in spirit and truth", "note": "Jesus identifies true worship with spirit and truth. It gives an interpretive complement to the commentary's refusal to reduce worship to bodily movement or spoken formula, while retaining worship as an enacted practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e6d090d1e4d0505d0d5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the people praying at the hour of incense", "note": "The whole assembled multitude prays outside during the incense hour. It offers a precise communal and timed liturgical counterpart to the commentary's formal prayer practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-13395f978bbc2ac6afe8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.64", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "released tongue blesses God", "note": "When Zechariah's mouth opens and his tongue is freed, he speaks blessing to God; this gives a concrete scene of the tongue becoming an instrument of praise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c15691e08de332bb14d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "self-exalting prayer as a failure", "note": "The Pharisee thanks God while boasting of his own righteousness; this gives a specific case where prayer's words do not express the inward devotion emphasized by the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3e3acfe6af31d4a17e8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "humble bodily supplication", "note": "The tax collector stands at a distance, will not lift his eyes, beats his breast, and asks God for mercy; this joins bodily posture and prayer with inward humility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ede24f88836fa1a7466", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bodily humility and a plea for mercy", "note": "The tax collector stands at a distance, lowers his eyes, beats his breast, and asks God for mercy; it supplies a concrete Christian scene in which bodily prayer and du'a-like petition are inseparable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ea9e06a0e71099dfcfce", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayerful humility and acceptance", "note": "Jesus says the tax collector, rather than the Pharisee, goes home justified; this makes the quality of the person praying matter, like the commentary's distinction between successful devotion and negligent prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ceff870a2210107e8280", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.2.37", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "night-and-day service with fasting and prayers", "note": "Anna serves God night and day through fasting and prayers. Her recurring discipline meets the commentary's abundant remembrance and continuing worship without reducing it to one isolated prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9607b1160e0bc7d5b63e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.22.41", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kneeling to pray", "note": "Jesus kneels on the Mount of Olives and prays; this is a direct bodily instance of the prayer movement described in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c5605bdba189d8a466e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.5.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "withdrawal for prayer", "note": "Jesus repeatedly withdraws to deserted places to pray; this is a specific secluded form of the prayer whose hidden dimension God knows in 87:7."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50e625f83a7a20a4b551", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.1.35", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer before dawn", "note": "Jesus rises while it is still dark and prays in a solitary place. This is a specific Christian scene for the commentary's praise and prayer before the sun's rising."}
{"connection_id": "BC-218e91366185c8ecf37e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.7.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus applies Isaiah to lip-service", "note": "Jesus quotes Isaiah's lips-honor/heart-distant charge against people who worship in vain; this Christian reading makes inward intention a test of outward religious practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b1723045e0e6e39fb12", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.15.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "lips honoring God; heart distant", "note": "Jesus applies Isaiah's lips-and-heart critique to a dispute about worship and tradition. It provides a Christian interpretive development of the commentary's contrast between tongue-level mention and wholehearted, attentive worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f7271136f42296faedc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.15.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "vain worship through human teaching", "note": "The continuation of Jesus' Isaiah citation calls worship vain when it teaches merely human precepts. It gives the explicit Christian continuation needed for the commentary's concern with heedless or merely external worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d346649eb1523439efa", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.26.39", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "falling on the face and praying", "note": "Jesus falls on his face and prays in Gethsemane, an explicit conjunction of prostrating posture and supplication comparable to the prayer movements named in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8817d8319f7c2a4e96b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "public prayer for display", "note": "Jesus warns against praying at street corners to be seen by people; this supplies a concrete public-prayer case for 87:7's distinction between the voiced and hidden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba776eb85d85cdc37fcd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "public prayer performed to be seen", "note": "Jesus criticizes prayer offered for public display. In conjunction with the commentary's treatment of jahr as audible prayer, it makes intention rather than volume the relevant ethical distinction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01a778c9dec8ef56484b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer in secret seen by God", "note": "Jesus directs the disciple to pray in a closed room, where the Father sees in secret; this complements 87:7's claim that God knows what is concealed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bffe25db88306339872c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer in secret; the Father seeing the hidden", "note": "Jesus directs prayer to the hidden place and grounds it in the Father's seeing what is hidden; this complements 87:7 as read in the commentary, where God knows both vocalized and concealed devotion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f36b85eb581375512a1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hallowing the Father's name", "note": "Jesus' prayer begins “hallowed be your name”; this places sanctification of God's name inside the prayer practice that follows."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ad90a0a98e179667e243", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hallowing the Father's name in prayer", "note": "Jesus' model prayer makes the hallowing of God's name its first petition, directly pairing prayer with the treatment of the divine name central to 87:1 and 87:15."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dae50544b4ee20e8b855", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "knees bow at Jesus' name", "note": "The hymn says every knee bends at Jesus' name; this joins bodily prostration to the honor of the Lord's name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a2fc20dab013066c176", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bowing every knee at a name", "note": "In its Christological setting, the verse makes the response to Jesus' name a universal bending of knees; it is a precise Christian meeting of named confession and the bodily prayer emphasized in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a6e659daa2f6b85fab3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "every tongue confesses the Lord", "note": "The hymn says every tongue confesses Jesus Christ as Lord; this makes spoken confession of the Lord's name a universal act of praise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-67cdee5e108b5d8ff832", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "every tongue confessing to God's glory", "note": "The verse completes the preceding knee-bending with every tongue's confession to the glory of God the Father. It gives the verbal side of the name-and-body meeting already present in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0caf09b105db5e28698b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.4.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer, supplication, thanksgiving", "note": "Paul joins prayer and supplication with thanksgiving in one act of address to God, closely echoing the commentary's salat as du'a and dhikr as praise and thanks."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d0d981a2793ca27d3bec", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.15.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "glorifying the Name and worship by all nations", "note": "The hymn asks who would not glorify the Lord's name and says all nations will come and worship. It directly pairs name-glorification with worship in a Christian doxological formula."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c09aa35558264fc5d55", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.5.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "incense identified as saints' prayers", "note": "The elders hold incense bowls that are the prayers of the saints; this gives a Christian vision the same concrete prayer-as-incense image as the psalmic prayer tradition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-483265c166b0d10307d8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.5.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "falling in worship; harps; bowls identified as prayers", "note": "The elders fall before the Lamb while holding harps and bowls of incense identified as the saints' prayers. This heavenly liturgy joins body, praise music, and prayer in a form especially relevant to the commentary's single devotional practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3cdd2fac24b8ee4aa13d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.10.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "calling on the Lord's name and salvation", "note": "Paul's citation makes calling on the Lord's name the path to salvation; it gives a specific New Testament meeting with the commentary's sequence of remembering the Name, praying, and flourishing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0365e8fc5445c31c588", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.12.1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "presenting bodies as living worship", "note": "Paul describes offering bodies as a living sacrifice and worship; it gives a direct Christian analogue to the commentary's claim that worship includes bodily action as well as verbal and intentional devotion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a36e3903a2f8e88ed6a6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.8.26", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wordless groans in prayer", "note": "The Spirit intercedes with groanings too deep for words; this gives a specific hidden, nonverbal dimension to the commentary's contrast between what is voiced and concealed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-850d113ca191ff9b57bb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.8.27", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God searches hearts", "note": "The one who searches hearts knows the Spirit's intention; this makes divine knowledge of what is inward explicit, as in 87:7."}
{"connection_id": "BC-101c140a2d24bb700e76", "ref": "1QS 10:1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blessing the Name at light and darkness", "note": "The Rule of the Community schedules blessing God's name at the turning points of light and darkness, a close sectarian-Jewish analogue to the commentary's praise before sunrise and sunset."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ee5f7d37d23485dc13d8", "ref": "Berakhot 26b", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "patriarchal derivation of fixed prayer times", "note": "The Talmud derives the three daily prayer services from Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. Its interpretive fixing of prayer in the day supplies a Jewish liturgical bridge to the commentary's praise at the sun's turning points."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4a69c648c905d3bb96f6", "ref": "Berakhot 31a", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Hannah as model of silent prayer", "note": "The Talmud reads Hannah's moving lips and unheard voice as a model for prayer that is articulated yet inaudible; this sharpens the commentary's distinction between vocalized and concealed prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-411ee08ae573a2b2e938", "ref": "Berakhot 31a", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Hannah's moving lips and unraised voice", "note": "The Talmud reads Hannah's silent, lip-moving prayer as a source for how the Amidah is voiced. That interpretation makes the commentary's audible prayer or recitation and its hidden dimension a concrete liturgical question."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4ff1cc94c2c7597b097", "ref": "Berakhot 3a", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Kaddish's sanctification of the great name", "note": "The Talmud's synagogue response asks that God's great name be blessed; this rabbinic liturgical formula makes praise of the name a communal prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-211a3e4faac5bb92b2ad", "ref": "Berakhot 40b", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "a blessing requires God's Name and kingship", "note": "The rule that a blessing without the Name and kingship is no blessing makes formal invocation of the divine Name a defining liturgical act, a useful rabbinic counterpart to 87:1 and 87:15."}
{"connection_id": "BC-364a1d888e774b60f5e4", "ref": "Kaddish, opening doxology", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "magnifying and sanctifying the great Name", "note": "Kaddish begins by asking that God's great name be magnified and sanctified; its congregational formula directly stands beside 87:1's command to glorify the Lord's name."}

