{"connection_id": "BC-9aa9d1ca91f4f7025acb", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.23", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "worship in spirit and truth", "note": "Jesus identifies true worship with spirit and truth. It gives an interpretive complement to the commentary's refusal to reduce worship to bodily movement or spoken formula, while retaining worship as an enacted practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e6d090d1e4d0505d0d5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the people praying at the hour of incense", "note": "The whole assembled multitude prays outside during the incense hour. It offers a precise communal and timed liturgical counterpart to the commentary's formal prayer practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-13395f978bbc2ac6afe8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.64", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "released tongue blesses God", "note": "When Zechariah's mouth opens and his tongue is freed, he speaks blessing to God; this gives a concrete scene of the tongue becoming an instrument of praise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c15691e08de332bb14d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "self-exalting prayer as a failure", "note": "The Pharisee thanks God while boasting of his own righteousness; this gives a specific case where prayer's words do not express the inward devotion emphasized by the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3e3acfe6af31d4a17e8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "humble bodily supplication", "note": "The tax collector stands at a distance, will not lift his eyes, beats his breast, and asks God for mercy; this joins bodily posture and prayer with inward humility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ede24f88836fa1a7466", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bodily humility and a plea for mercy", "note": "The tax collector stands at a distance, lowers his eyes, beats his breast, and asks God for mercy; it supplies a concrete Christian scene in which bodily prayer and du'a-like petition are inseparable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ea9e06a0e71099dfcfce", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.18.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayerful humility and acceptance", "note": "Jesus says the tax collector, rather than the Pharisee, goes home justified; this makes the quality of the person praying matter, like the commentary's distinction between successful devotion and negligent prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ceff870a2210107e8280", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.2.37", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "night-and-day service with fasting and prayers", "note": "Anna serves God night and day through fasting and prayers. Her recurring discipline meets the commentary's abundant remembrance and continuing worship without reducing it to one isolated prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9607b1160e0bc7d5b63e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.22.41", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kneeling to pray", "note": "Jesus kneels on the Mount of Olives and prays; this is a direct bodily instance of the prayer movement described in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c5605bdba189d8a466e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.5.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "withdrawal for prayer", "note": "Jesus repeatedly withdraws to deserted places to pray; this is a specific secluded form of the prayer whose hidden dimension God knows in 87:7."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50e625f83a7a20a4b551", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.1.35", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer before dawn", "note": "Jesus rises while it is still dark and prays in a solitary place. This is a specific Christian scene for the commentary's praise and prayer before the sun's rising."}
{"connection_id": "BC-218e91366185c8ecf37e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.7.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus applies Isaiah to lip-service", "note": "Jesus quotes Isaiah's lips-honor/heart-distant charge against people who worship in vain; this Christian reading makes inward intention a test of outward religious practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b1723045e0e6e39fb12", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.15.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "lips honoring God; heart distant", "note": "Jesus applies Isaiah's lips-and-heart critique to a dispute about worship and tradition. It provides a Christian interpretive development of the commentary's contrast between tongue-level mention and wholehearted, attentive worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f7271136f42296faedc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.15.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "vain worship through human teaching", "note": "The continuation of Jesus' Isaiah citation calls worship vain when it teaches merely human precepts. It gives the explicit Christian continuation needed for the commentary's concern with heedless or merely external worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d346649eb1523439efa", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.26.39", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "falling on the face and praying", "note": "Jesus falls on his face and prays in Gethsemane, an explicit conjunction of prostrating posture and supplication comparable to the prayer movements named in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8817d8319f7c2a4e96b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "public prayer for display", "note": "Jesus warns against praying at street corners to be seen by people; this supplies a concrete public-prayer case for 87:7's distinction between the voiced and hidden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba776eb85d85cdc37fcd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "public prayer performed to be seen", "note": "Jesus criticizes prayer offered for public display. In conjunction with the commentary's treatment of jahr as audible prayer, it makes intention rather than volume the relevant ethical distinction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01a778c9dec8ef56484b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer in secret seen by God", "note": "Jesus directs the disciple to pray in a closed room, where the Father sees in secret; this complements 87:7's claim that God knows what is concealed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bffe25db88306339872c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer in secret; the Father seeing the hidden", "note": "Jesus directs prayer to the hidden place and grounds it in the Father's seeing what is hidden; this complements 87:7 as read in the commentary, where God knows both vocalized and concealed devotion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f36b85eb581375512a1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hallowing the Father's name", "note": "Jesus' prayer begins “hallowed be your name”; this places sanctification of God's name inside the prayer practice that follows."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ad90a0a98e179667e243", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hallowing the Father's name in prayer", "note": "Jesus' model prayer makes the hallowing of God's name its first petition, directly pairing prayer with the treatment of the divine name central to 87:1 and 87:15."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dae50544b4ee20e8b855", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "knees bow at Jesus' name", "note": "The hymn says every knee bends at Jesus' name; this joins bodily prostration to the honor of the Lord's name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a2fc20dab013066c176", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bowing every knee at a name", "note": "In its Christological setting, the verse makes the response to Jesus' name a universal bending of knees; it is a precise Christian meeting of named confession and the bodily prayer emphasized in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a6e659daa2f6b85fab3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "every tongue confesses the Lord", "note": "The hymn says every tongue confesses Jesus Christ as Lord; this makes spoken confession of the Lord's name a universal act of praise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-67cdee5e108b5d8ff832", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "every tongue confessing to God's glory", "note": "The verse completes the preceding knee-bending with every tongue's confession to the glory of God the Father. It gives the verbal side of the name-and-body meeting already present in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0caf09b105db5e28698b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.4.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer, supplication, thanksgiving", "note": "Paul joins prayer and supplication with thanksgiving in one act of address to God, closely echoing the commentary's salat as du'a and dhikr as praise and thanks."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d0d981a2793ca27d3bec", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.15.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "glorifying the Name and worship by all nations", "note": "The hymn asks who would not glorify the Lord's name and says all nations will come and worship. It directly pairs name-glorification with worship in a Christian doxological formula."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c09aa35558264fc5d55", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.5.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "incense identified as saints' prayers", "note": "The elders hold incense bowls that are the prayers of the saints; this gives a Christian vision the same concrete prayer-as-incense image as the psalmic prayer tradition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-483265c166b0d10307d8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.5.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "falling in worship; harps; bowls identified as prayers", "note": "The elders fall before the Lamb while holding harps and bowls of incense identified as the saints' prayers. This heavenly liturgy joins body, praise music, and prayer in a form especially relevant to the commentary's single devotional practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3cdd2fac24b8ee4aa13d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.10.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "calling on the Lord's name and salvation", "note": "Paul's citation makes calling on the Lord's name the path to salvation; it gives a specific New Testament meeting with the commentary's sequence of remembering the Name, praying, and flourishing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0365e8fc5445c31c588", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.12.1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "presenting bodies as living worship", "note": "Paul describes offering bodies as a living sacrifice and worship; it gives a direct Christian analogue to the commentary's claim that worship includes bodily action as well as verbal and intentional devotion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a36e3903a2f8e88ed6a6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.8.26", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wordless groans in prayer", "note": "The Spirit intercedes with groanings too deep for words; this gives a specific hidden, nonverbal dimension to the commentary's contrast between what is voiced and concealed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-850d113ca191ff9b57bb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.8.27", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God searches hearts", "note": "The one who searches hearts knows the Spirit's intention; this makes divine knowledge of what is inward explicit, as in 87:7."}
{"connection_id": "BC-101c140a2d24bb700e76", "ref": "1QS 10:1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blessing the Name at light and darkness", "note": "The Rule of the Community schedules blessing God's name at the turning points of light and darkness, a close sectarian-Jewish analogue to the commentary's praise before sunrise and sunset."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ee5f7d37d23485dc13d8", "ref": "Berakhot 26b", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "patriarchal derivation of fixed prayer times", "note": "The Talmud derives the three daily prayer services from Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. Its interpretive fixing of prayer in the day supplies a Jewish liturgical bridge to the commentary's praise at the sun's turning points."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4a69c648c905d3bb96f6", "ref": "Berakhot 31a", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Hannah as model of silent prayer", "note": "The Talmud reads Hannah's moving lips and unheard voice as a model for prayer that is articulated yet inaudible; this sharpens the commentary's distinction between vocalized and concealed prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-411ee08ae573a2b2e938", "ref": "Berakhot 31a", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Hannah's moving lips and unraised voice", "note": "The Talmud reads Hannah's silent, lip-moving prayer as a source for how the Amidah is voiced. That interpretation makes the commentary's audible prayer or recitation and its hidden dimension a concrete liturgical question."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4ff1cc94c2c7597b097", "ref": "Berakhot 3a", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Kaddish's sanctification of the great name", "note": "The Talmud's synagogue response asks that God's great name be blessed; this rabbinic liturgical formula makes praise of the name a communal prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-211a3e4faac5bb92b2ad", "ref": "Berakhot 40b", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "a blessing requires God's Name and kingship", "note": "The rule that a blessing without the Name and kingship is no blessing makes formal invocation of the divine Name a defining liturgical act, a useful rabbinic counterpart to 87:1 and 87:15."}
{"connection_id": "BC-364a1d888e774b60f5e4", "ref": "Kaddish, opening doxology", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "magnifying and sanctifying the great Name", "note": "Kaddish begins by asking that God's great name be magnified and sanctified; its congregational formula directly stands beside 87:1's command to glorify the Lord's name."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-63cf00cfcd6632c4fca6", "ref": "Mishnah Berakhot 2:2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Torah reading, Shema, directing the heart", "note": "The Mishnah asks whether someone already reading Torah fulfils the Shema when its time comes, and makes the answer turn on directing the heart. It clarifies the commentary's claim that recitation can be remembrance and worship only with intention."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e9988a3d58815527367", "ref": "Mishnah Berakhot 4:1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "daily prayer times", "note": "The Mishnah sets times for morning, afternoon, and evening prayer; this gives a concrete Jewish schedule for repeated prayer as a regular practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6953bad3f7788f1ff612", "ref": "Mishnah Berakhot 4:4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "prayer as supplication", "note": "Rabbi Eliezer's dictum that a prayer made fixed is not supplication gives a Jewish interpretive counterpart to the commentary's explicit gloss that salat is du'a, while resisting prayer as empty routine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d33e064e6067bc74c09", "ref": "Mishnah Berakhot 5:1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "solemn intention before prayer", "note": "The Mishnah says the pious waited before praying so they could direct their hearts to God; this joins inward intention to the bodily act, both included in the commentary's account of worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c40c1024eba06869b1a", "ref": "Mishnah Berakhot 5:1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "directing the heart before prayer", "note": "The early pietists pause before prayer in order to direct their hearts to their Father in heaven; this gives a concrete rabbinic practice for the commentary's inclusion of intention within tesbih and salat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2c581e66cfa9a45f16fa", "ref": "Mishnah Tamid 5:1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "public Torah and Shema recitation in temple liturgy", "note": "The daily temple order combines blessings with recitation of Torah passages and the Shema. It gives a concrete Jewish liturgical setting for the commentary's claim that scriptural reading is part of remembrance and worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-469fe190f6ebd20f96ca", "ref": "Mishnah Yoma 3:8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "explicit Name; kneeling, prostration, blessing", "note": "On hearing the explicit Name from the high priest, priests and people kneel, prostrate, fall on their faces, and bless the Name; this rabbinic liturgy tightly combines the Name and the bodily acts named in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-492e1f557bea649bd80b", "ref": "Sirach 39:35", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "whole heart and voice; blessing the Lord's Name", "note": "Ben Sira tells hearers to give thanks with all heart and voice and bless the Lord's name. It is an unusually close Jewish synthesis of inward intent, vocal praise, and the Name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a08576d25fc77bf78928", "ref": "Sirach 50:17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "falling face-down before God Most High", "note": "The assembled people fall on their faces to worship the Almighty, God Most High; it brings together the commentary's epithet, public liturgy, and prostrating body."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f53f1fca0653eedfb3fa", "ref": "Ta'anit 2a", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "service of the heart interpreted as prayer", "note": "The Talmud asks what service is performed with the heart and answers, prayer. This interpretation directly illuminates the commentary's claim that worship includes intention as well as its audible and bodily forms."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3b0f990df225bd5e46a", "ref": "Tobit 12:6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thanksgiving made known to the living", "note": "Raphael tells Tobit and Tobias to bless God and make God's goodness known among the living; this is a specific verbal enactment of praise and remembrance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2810f0e3adc65c516348", "ref": "WLC:1Chr.16.29", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "name-praise, offering, and worship", "note": "The worshiper ascribes glory due YHWH's name, brings an offering, and bows in holy array; this is a specific conjunction of praise and bodily ritual."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d02c8af7c124e7e7dd48", "ref": "WLC:1Chr.16.29", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "glory due the Name; offering and worship", "note": "The verse moves from giving YHWH the glory due his name to bringing an offering and worshipping, closely matching the commentary's treatment of praise of the Name and formal worship as one practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5648656909897691f517", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.36", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Elijah's spoken prayer at the offering", "note": "At the evening offering Elijah addresses YHWH by name and asks that Israel know he is God; this is a public, voiced prayer embedded in ritual worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-075c4ad037375dbae34f", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.37", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "answer that turns hearts back", "note": "Elijah asks YHWH to hear so the people will know him and their hearts will turn back; this joins invocation, response, and inward return in a single prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-df36367dfc3bc8e497e3", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.39", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "falling on faces; spoken confession of God", "note": "The people fall on their faces and voice a confession of YHWH; the verse joins the physical prostration and verbal God-remembrance that the commentary treats as a single act."}
{"connection_id": "BC-32722b7b4d248e5c6bc7", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.8.22", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "standing before the altar; spreading hands toward heaven", "note": "Solomon stands before the altar in the assembled people's presence and spreads his hands heavenward. It supplies a specific biblical prayer posture alongside the commentary's standing and supplicatory movements."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d4b60386be6795c6569", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.8.30", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "supplication heard; divine forgiveness", "note": "Solomon asks God to hear prayer and supplication and to forgive. The verse gives a direct biblical meeting between human du'a-like appeal and the divine cleansing or forgiveness side of the commentary's 14–15 linkage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ee74875642870724ecf8", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.8.39", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God knows the heart", "note": "Solomon asks God to hear from heaven and act because God alone knows each human heart; this gives a specific parallel to knowing what is hidden in 87:7."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d6c070253bcffe88351", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.8.54", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kneeling and hands extended", "note": "Solomon finishes his prayer and supplication while kneeling with hands stretched toward heaven; this makes the commentary's bodily definition of prayer concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b012378a5a47fc0f064d", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.1.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "inaudible prayer, moving lips", "note": "Hannah prays in her heart while her lips move and her voice is not heard; this concretely distinguishes audible prayer from hidden devotion, matching 87:7's public and concealed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ae7a8a3b45533ab6b661", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.1.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lips moving; voice unheard; inward prayer", "note": "Hannah speaks in her heart while her lips move and her voice is not heard; the contrast supplies a precise counterpart to the commentary's open vocal prayer or recitation and God's knowledge of what is hidden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9dab0805fe24c3d8a04e", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.7.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prostration after the prayer", "note": "After the fire and glory appear, Israel bows face to the ground and worships; this gives a specific bodily response after prayer and offering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b598d9142b2930e5f5bf", "ref": "WLC:Dan.3.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "forced prostration to an image; fiery punishment", "note": "The verse orders bodily falling and worship under threat of a burning furnace. Alongside the commentary's prostration as Lord-directed prayer and its fire imagery, it makes the object and moral character of bodily worship decisive."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c94f8c7af59729325c85", "ref": "WLC:Dan.6.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kneeling prayer with thanks", "note": "Daniel kneels three times daily, prays, and gives thanks to God; this is a specific repeated bodily practice joining prayer and praise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f1fd4daa50b2bfb25460", "ref": "WLC:Dan.6.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic צלי, prayer", "note": "Daniel's Aramaic form from צלי means “pray” beside the act of kneeling and giving thanks; it is a close Semitic counterpart to the commentary's ص ل و label, though the etymological relation to Arabic ṣalāt is uncertain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd69ba4cb226fca25828", "ref": "WLC:Dan.6.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kneeling, prayer, thanksgiving, three daily times", "note": "Daniel kneels three times a day, prays, and gives thanks before his God, furnishing a specific counterpart to prayer as bodily movement, supplication, praise, and a recurring practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e6e0c8425a9977f5c9ee", "ref": "WLC:Dan.6.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل و (B002/B003); Biblical Aramaic מְצַלֵּא", "note": "The commentary labels ص ل و as B002/B003. Daniel's Aramaic מְצַלֵּא, “praying,” is a close Aramaic-Syriac cognate form; its repeated kneeling and thanksgiving make the linguistic meeting concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-acca6f1b1bc5061b8d66", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.21", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "song not forgotten from descendants' mouths", "note": "The covenant song is to remain unforgotten from Israel's mouths; it supplies a concrete scriptural analogue to the commentary's movement from being made to recite to not forgetting and then reminding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5cb64fe487cc4ff5c9b4", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "proclaiming the Name; ascribing greatness", "note": "87:1 commands glorification of the Lord's name; Moses says that he will proclaim YHWH's name and calls his hearers to ascribe greatness to God, joining the two acts in one formula."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f1b6e8345954676d55e8", "ref": "WLC:Deut.6.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "words continually spoken", "note": "Israel is told to speak God's words at home, on the road, at lying down and rising; this is a specific scriptural practice of remembrance circulating on the tongue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-06f66ac75c70d9aa83e9", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.18", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ذ ك ر (B004/B005); Hebrew זכר", "note": "The commentary labels ذ ك ر as B004/B005 and develops remembering the Lord. Deuteronomy's זכר, “remember,” directs Israel to remember YHWH as the giver of power, a cognate used for faithful recollection rather than merely saying a word."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6709e92028ce7762c80a", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.5.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "heart and mouth before God", "note": "The speaker is warned not to rush words or heart in prayer before God; this adds a concrete caution about outward speech and inward attention to the commentary's account of vocal and hidden worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89dd95dd4bb87867b8aa", "ref": "WLC:Exod.20.24", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew זכר at the altar", "note": "YHWH says he will come and bless wherever he causes his name to be remembered; the Hebrew אזכיר uses זכר, cognate with the commentary's ذ ك ر label, in a worship setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-66987e3d1292cee39917", "ref": "WLC:Exod.20.24", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "name remembered where worship occurs", "note": "God promises presence and blessing at the altar where his name is remembered; this ties remembrance of the name to embodied worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6f0f12faeacf49d574f", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "name as memorial for generations", "note": "YHWH says his name is for ever and his memorial through all generations; this makes the divine name itself an enduring object of remembrance beside 87:1 and 87:15."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6771c58df8b3951dcedf", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew זכר, the divine memorial", "note": "The Hebrew זִכְרִי (“my memorial”) is from זכר, cognate with the commentary's ذ ك ر label; here God's name is the memorial kept across generations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-262a4b84c0205fa1d5e7", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.36.25", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God sprinkling clean water and cleansing", "note": "Ezekiel depicts God cleansing the people from impurity. It does not itself define prayer, but it supplies the divine purification pole required by the commentary's linking of 87:14 to God's purifying salat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72fb64729ead536ea24f", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.6.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic צלי, prayer for the king", "note": "The Aramaic form מְצַלַּיִן means “they pray” for the king and his sons; it is a close Semitic counterpart to the commentary's ص ل و label, though the etymological relation to Arabic ṣalāt is uncertain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de6d7d029509b61ed6e6", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.6.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer beside offerings", "note": "The decree asks that temple offerings be made to heaven's God and prayers offered for the king; this places bodily cult and supplication together."}

