{"connection_id": "BC-c16035ae82eabfbd25dd", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Torah reading, confession, worship", "note": "The people stand to read Torah and then confess and worship; this concretely parallels the commentary's claim that recitation, admonitory remembrance, and prayer belong to the same devotional work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ff1160ea71a4747a21b", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "standing blessing of the name", "note": "The Levites tell the assembly to stand and bless YHWH forever, then bless his glorious name; this joins bodily worship and praise of the name as in the commentary's opening and closing act."}
{"connection_id": "BC-321c3ac56e92512455e7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.100.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thanksgiving, praise, blessing the Name", "note": "Entering God's courts entails thanksgiving and praise, then giving thanks to him and blessing his name. The verse closely joins the commentary's dhikr as praise and thanks with public worship of the Name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-161c092623ca6dd3d371", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praise and not forgetting", "note": "The speaker blesses YHWH and tells his own soul not to forget God's benefits; this links praise with deliberate remembrance, including the commentary's concern with forgetting."}
