 {"connection_id":"BC-3ef4ea40fff3fbe585bf","ref":"SBLGNT:John.11.42","status":"rejected","reason":"Jesus says he spoke for the crowd's belief; this qualifies a claim about public prayer's motive, but the base makes no claim that voiced prayer is wrong.","paragraphs":[44],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.11.42"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9aa9d1ca91f4f7025acb","ref":"SBLGNT:John.4.23","status":"rejected","reason":"Worship in spirit and truth addresses a different dispute about place and true worship, with no divine-name, recitation or bodily-prayer sequence in the cited verse.","paragraphs":[43,44],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.4.23"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8e6d090d1e4d0505d0d5","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.1.10","status":"rejected","reason":"The people pray during incense at a stated hour, but the verse lacks the section's name-praise and recitation motifs; Mal 1:11 combines time, name and offering more closely.","paragraphs":[45],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.1.10"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-13395f978bbc2ac6afe8","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.1.64","status":"rejected","reason":"Zechariah's freed tongue blesses God, but this isolated restoration scene neither mentions the divine name nor connects praise with prayer.","paragraphs":[43],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.1.64"],"annotations":[]}
